WEBVTT

0
00:00:06.969 --> 00:00:11.229
The design phase can be broken down into 8 individual steps.

1
00:00:11.569 --> 00:00:15.210
Each step helps instructional designers make specific

2
00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:17.399
decisions about what the learners will achieve,

3
00:00:17.770 --> 00:00:18.690
how they will learn,

4
00:00:18.969 --> 00:00:21.909
and how their progress will be supported and measured.

5
00:00:22.489 --> 00:00:23.370
Step 1.

6
00:00:24.229 --> 00:00:26.190
Set learning objectives.

7
00:00:26.510 --> 00:00:30.159
Learning objectives define what learners should be able to demonstrate

8
00:00:30.579 --> 00:00:32.220
after completing the instruction.

9
00:00:32.909 --> 00:00:37.189
Effective learning objectives always use observable and measurable verbs,

10
00:00:37.509 --> 00:00:39.750
making it possible to evaluate whether the

11
00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:42.270
learners have achieved the intended outcomes.

12
00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:44.049
Step 2,

13
00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:47.009
select instructional strategies and theories.

14
00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:51.659
The next step is to select instructional strategies and learning theories.

15
00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:54.970
These decisions guide how learners will understand,

16
00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:55.880
practice,

17
00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:57.700
and remember the information.

18
00:00:58.159 --> 00:00:58.919
Step 3,

19
00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:01.220
plan and sequence the content.

20
00:01:01.560 --> 00:01:04.980
The content must then be planned and placed in logical order.

21
00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.720
Learners should receive information in a sequence

22
00:01:07.720 --> 00:01:10.180
that is easy to understand and follow.

23
00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:12.440
Step 4,

24
00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:14.040
choose teaching methods.

25
00:01:14.449 --> 00:01:17.290
The next step is to choose how the content will be taught.

26
00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:19.449
Teaching methods may include videos,

27
00:01:19.529 --> 00:01:20.080
slides,

28
00:01:20.319 --> 00:01:20.760
discussions,

29
00:01:20.849 --> 00:01:21.720
demonstrations,

30
00:01:21.809 --> 00:01:22.610
explanations,

31
00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:24.069
or guided practice.

32
00:01:24.489 --> 00:01:25.470
Step 5

33
00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:27.440
design learning activities.

34
00:01:27.769 --> 00:01:30.209
Learning activities allow learners to apply,

35
00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:30.809
practice,

36
00:01:30.889 --> 00:01:32.839
and reinforce what they have learned.

37
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:33.809
For example,

38
00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:36.970
learners could arrange the steps of a task in the correct order,

39
00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:39.050
complete a practice exercise,

40
00:01:39.449 --> 00:01:41.000
solve a realistic problem,

41
00:01:41.169 --> 00:01:42.900
or participate in a simulation.

42
00:01:43.809 --> 00:01:44.959
Step 6,

43
00:01:45.319 --> 00:01:46.900
plan assessments.

44
00:01:47.199 --> 00:01:51.040
Instructional designers must also decide how learning will be measured.

45
00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.120
Assessments may include quizzes,

46
00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:53.709
tests,

47
00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:54.400
projects,

48
00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:55.269
demonstrations,

49
00:01:55.319 --> 00:01:56.720
or practical tasks.

50
00:01:57.919 --> 00:01:58.790
Step 7,

51
00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:00.739
select media and tools.

52
00:02:01.069 --> 00:02:03.889
Instructional designers must also identify the media,

53
00:02:03.949 --> 00:02:07.489
technologies and resources required to deliver the instruction.

54
00:02:07.949 --> 00:02:09.669
These may include an LMS,

55
00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:10.589
videos,

56
00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:11.470
applications,

57
00:02:11.589 --> 00:02:12.190
handouts,

58
00:02:12.339 --> 00:02:13.410
interactive tools,

59
00:02:13.630 --> 00:02:14.869
or physical equipment.

60
00:02:15.830 --> 00:02:16.740
Step 8,

61
00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:19.419
plan feedback and learner support.

62
00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:23.279
The final step is to plan how learners will receive feedback,

63
00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:23.910
guidance,

64
00:02:23.919 --> 00:02:24.889
and support.

65
00:02:25.279 --> 00:02:26.199
To achieve this,

66
00:02:26.479 --> 00:02:29.479
the curriculum may integrate direct instructor insights,

67
00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:31.160
automated quiz responses,

68
00:02:31.279 --> 00:02:32.110
helpful hints,

69
00:02:32.199 --> 00:02:33.500
real world examples,

70
00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:35.759
and additional practice opportunities.